21:05.780 --> 21:10.080
The historical states, the ancestral cells, are physically annihilated.

21:10.480 --> 21:14.000
They are transformed out of existence by the very process of development.

21:14.100 --> 21:16.080
So all the scientists have are terminal states.

21:16.200 --> 21:20.140
They just have a massive pile of current, present-day descendant cells.

21:20.140 --> 21:21.100
It's just the endpoints.

21:21.220 --> 21:21.360
Yeah.

21:21.460 --> 21:29.040
And they take these cells and use single-cell RNA sequencing to get a high-dimensional snapshot of what genes are turned on in each cell right now.

21:29.280 --> 21:30.360
But they need the history.

21:30.480 --> 21:30.620
Right.

21:30.680 --> 21:31.240
They need the tree.

21:31.440 --> 21:34.500
So they use computational models, like one called TreeVe.

21:34.500 --> 21:44.600
And what TreeVe does is it looks at the tiny, surviving distinctions in the RNA of the present-day cells, and it mathematically infers the historical trajectory.

21:45.140 --> 21:51.800
It reconstructs the family tree of cell divisions that must have occurred to produce these specific terminal states.

21:52.040 --> 21:54.200
It is a pure application of Flyxion's theory.

21:55.020 --> 21:57.260
The scientists are not looking at invariant objects.

21:57.260 --> 22:04.280
The original cells are gone, but the biological system preserve the distinctions through the transformations of development.

22:04.720 --> 22:13.000
The algorithm acts as the reconstruction operator R, using the present-day molecular traces as memory to constrain the reconstruction of the past.

22:13.200 --> 22:15.580
The history itself is a computational resource.

22:16.400 --> 22:19.860
The present state is unintelligible without recovering the trajectory.

22:19.980 --> 22:20.480
Exactly.

