24:29.640 --> 24:35.680
There are deep, stable valleys and basins, surrounded by incredibly steep, jagged, unstable hills.

24:36.340 --> 24:40.280
This is a visual model of what Flyxion calls the persistence field.

24:40.540 --> 24:44.480
It looks like a gravity well, or, you know, a topographic map of a mountain range.

24:44.480 --> 24:46.300
The geometry works similarly.

24:46.960 --> 24:52.060
To understand the persistence field, we have to fundamentally redefine information and entropy.

24:52.500 --> 24:54.020
Okay, let's start with information.

24:54.020 --> 24:59.380
In the 20th century, Claude Shannon gave us classical information theory.

25:00.180 --> 25:06.040
We learn to think of information as symbols, bits, ones and zeros transmitted over a wire.

25:06.360 --> 25:09.400
We measure information by the predictability of the symbols.

25:09.600 --> 25:10.620
But Flyxion says no.

25:11.040 --> 25:12.200
Information is not a symbol.

25:12.660 --> 25:14.500
Information is recoverable distinction.

25:14.500 --> 25:17.980
Which is a subtle, but profound shift.

25:17.980 --> 25:24.100
A distinction only carries information to the extent that it remains available after transformation and reconstruction.

25:24.100 --> 25:25.860
Let's illustrate that.

25:26.340 --> 25:28.580
Say I write you a detailed letter and mail it.

25:29.260 --> 25:39.140
If the postal system drops it in a river and the ink washes away so thoroughly that absolutely no reconstruction operator can read it, then mathematically, no information was transmitted.

25:39.360 --> 25:39.600
None.

25:39.900 --> 25:42.360
Information is not an intrinsic property of the paper.

25:42.800 --> 25:44.260
It is a relational property.

25:44.460 --> 25:46.200
It is entirely about what survives.

25:46.540 --> 25:49.140
And if information gets flipped, entropy gets flipped too.

25:49.140 --> 25:49.460
Right.

25:49.820 --> 25:53.020
We are taught in high school physics that entropy is a measure of disorder.

25:53.820 --> 25:59.000
The classic example, a shattered egg on the floor, has higher entropy than a whole egg on the counter.

25:59.400 --> 26:04.140
Flyxion redefines entropy as the loss of recoverable distinction.

26:04.560 --> 26:05.400
Okay, so the egg.

26:05.400 --> 26:09.580
Yes, the shattered egg has higher entropy, but not just because it looks messy.

26:10.100 --> 26:19.400
It has higher entropy because the structural distinctions that made it a functioning biological egg are now vastly more difficult, practically impossible to reconstruct.

26:19.840 --> 26:20.220
Ah.

26:20.680 --> 26:25.540
On our topographical map, entropy is motion up those steep, unstable hills.

26:25.760 --> 26:26.040
Yes.

26:26.540 --> 26:32.340
You are moving into regions where reconstruction fails, where distinctions are lost to ambiguity.

26:32.720 --> 26:33.180
And repair.

26:33.180 --> 26:40.240
Repair, on the other hand, is motion down the hills into the deep basins, where distinctions are stable and easily recoverable.

26:40.500 --> 26:45.780
Here's where it gets really interesting, because this totally reframes how we view science and human reasoning.

26:46.860 --> 26:53.260
If entropy is the loss of distinction, and repair is the recovery of distinction, then what is a scientific theory?

26:53.600 --> 26:54.660
It's a repair operator.

26:54.940 --> 26:55.200
Right.

26:55.480 --> 27:02.260
An explanation, whether it's Einstein's theory of relativity or just you explaining why you were late to work, is essentially a repair operator.

27:02.260 --> 27:07.160
Explanations are higher-order repair processes operating within distinction space.

27:07.500 --> 27:08.720
Let's break that down mechanically.

27:09.360 --> 27:12.320
Imagine an ancient astronomer looking at the night sky.

27:13.320 --> 27:17.060
They observe a planet, say, Mars, moving weirdly.

27:17.420 --> 27:18.360
Retrograde motion.

27:18.480 --> 27:18.640
Yeah.

27:19.180 --> 27:22.760
Most nights it moves east, but occasionally it stops and moves west.

27:23.300 --> 27:25.340
The astronomer plots these dots on a chart.

27:26.360 --> 27:30.620
Without a theory, this data is highly entropic in Fuchsian's sense.

27:30.620 --> 27:31.420
It's fragmented.

27:31.560 --> 27:32.340
Doesn't make sense.

27:32.540 --> 27:38.720
The distinctions between the dots are hard to predict, and if you lose a page of data, you can't reconstruct where Mars was.

27:39.000 --> 27:43.480
The data points are sitting high up on the unstable hills of the persistence field.

27:43.600 --> 27:50.380
But then, Johannes Kepler comes along and introduces the theory of planetary motion, and later Newton introduces gravity.

27:51.040 --> 27:53.860
Suddenly, this theory acts as a global repair operator.

27:53.940 --> 27:54.980
It connects everything.

27:54.980 --> 27:59.400
It draws an elegant elliptical pathway that connects all those fragmented dots.

27:59.800 --> 28:06.040
It drastically lowers the entropy of the data because it creates a robust mathematical pathway to reconstruct those distinctions.

28:06.340 --> 28:06.660
Exactly.

28:06.760 --> 28:13.280
If I lose a month of observations, I can use the theory of gravity to perfectly reconstruct exactly where Mars was.

28:13.820 --> 28:16.220
The explanation repaired the structural relationships.

28:16.220 --> 28:18.260
It didn't just describe the dots.

28:18.420 --> 28:20.440
It restored their structural alignment.

28:21.120 --> 28:26.640
And the text highlights a fascinating, cutting-edge modern equivalent of this with AI flow matching.

28:27.000 --> 28:27.260
Right.

28:27.360 --> 28:30.180
The 2026 work by Moorhead and colleagues.

